1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:13,200
First, put one question inside a Warp: an isolated, reversible
piece of work. It is a project boundary, not a mathematical
force. We test the animation before building its explanation.

2
00:00:14,150 --> 00:00:26,131
This approach assigns four quadrant colors to sampled points.
Greater resolution reveals finer boundaries. Here, zeta's output
origin moves smoothly from zero to one.

3
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:37,252
The brush stays upright as it follows a circle. Changing direction changes the colors left behind, while new marks overwrite old ones.

4
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:54,200
Wegert's phase plots explain the color view. Lester's work
places zeta's a-points in an established research area. We
connect those ideas to our earlier two-state switch, using
classical prediction and Newton correction.

5
00:00:55,150 --> 00:01:12,008
Platt and Trudgian provide rigorous finite checks. Lamzouri's
preprint presents a proportion bound; OpenAI reports a zero-free
region and formalization. These concern zeta zeros, not a
certificate for our moving nonzero targets.

6
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:25,224
We follow the same root as the target moves. Where zeta prime is
nonzero, this formula gives its direction and speed. Smaller
steps and corrections help the computation stay on track.

7
00:01:28,150 --> 00:01:42,128
Independent checks find two paths leaving the right edge before
the target reaches one. The third reaches a finite one-point.
Leaving our window means the view ended, not that the
mathematical branch stopped.

8
00:01:44,150 --> 00:01:59,200
Color shows phase. Continuation follows a solution. Higher
precision checks numerical stability. Analytic estimates would
be needed for a certificate. These methods help separate tracked
connections from artifacts of switching or coarse pixels.

9
00:02:00,150 --> 00:02:14,200
Our conjecture required all three paths to remain inside the
window. The computed exits give numerical counterevidence. A
rigorous rejection would require certified continuation bounds.
We keep the original window.

10
00:02:15,150 --> 00:02:28,850
One-points are not zeta zeros. Even three successful paths would
not exclude an unseen zero off the critical line. A proof of
Riemann needs a global constraint, beyond what these pictures
establish.

11
00:02:30,150 --> 00:02:45,382
This approach colors each sampled point by its output quadrant
relative to a chosen origin. Greater rendering resolution
reveals finer boundaries. Colors indicate direction; at a zero,
the phase is undefined.

12
00:02:50,150 --> 00:03:08,029
Our finite computation found two paths leaving the original
window and one reaching a one-point. Independent numerical
checks support those observations. They do not certify every
point between samples, and they do not show that the exiting
branches disappear.

13
00:03:09,150 --> 00:03:27,200
The next precise task is to certify those three paths: prove
uniqueness in overlapping boxes, exclude a zero derivative, and
certify the two boundary crossings. That would turn selected
numerical observations into a bounded mathematical certificate.

14
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:47,200
Boseman and Pauli already studied this continuation family.
Complex target paths offer broader exploration. Speiser links
Riemann to excluding derivative zeros throughout an entire half-
strip. Our three paths do not cover that obligation; a global
proof is still missing.

15
00:03:48,150 --> 00:04:08,862
There is a genuine bridge to primes: explicit formulas connect
weighted prime counts with zeta zeros. Our proposed next
experiment would reconstruct that count from finitely many zeros
and certify the omitted error. A useful result would be a
rigorously bounded approximation to prime distribution.

16
00:04:12,150 --> 00:04:34,302
From zero through one hundred, every composite has a prime
divisor no larger than ten. Testing two, three, five and seven
therefore gives a complete classification: twenty-five primes.
Each label has an exact divisibility certificate. Violet
displays the proved answers; it is not a color-based prediction.

17
00:04:40,150 --> 00:05:02,998
Phase colors can guide us toward candidate zeros. Here the model
function is simply z. Around the unit circle, it never vanishes
and its phase makes one full turn, proving one zero inside. For
zeta, rigorous bounds and complete zero counts are still needed.
Brush trails alone do not supply them.

18
00:05:08,650 --> 00:05:14,217
This idea starts with a four-color quadrant brush at each point.

19
00:05:14,217 --> 00:05:18,772
Its trail changes with direction, overlap, rotation, and resolution.

20
00:05:18,772 --> 00:05:21,302
That makes the geometry visible.

21
00:05:21,302 --> 00:05:28,387
But a surviving pixel color is not, by itself, an arithmetic certificate of primality.

22
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:36,857
Z three means the third zeta zero, not twenty-three or a prime label.

23
00:05:36,857 --> 00:05:45,798
For the first hundred zero indices, negative starting slopes occurred at fourteen prime indices and thirty-five composite indices.

24
00:05:45,798 --> 00:05:48,281
Eleven prime indices sloped upward.

25
00:05:48,281 --> 00:05:53,248
Both signs mix the classes, so this proposed rule fails.

26
00:05:55,275 --> 00:05:59,900
The proposed denominator, g of n, simplifies exactly to n.

27
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:05,912
Substituting it into zeta's series leaves the same function where that series converges.

28
00:06:05,912 --> 00:06:10,999
The half inside g does not set s to one half.

29
00:06:10,999 --> 00:06:17,473
And ninety-seven and composite ninety-nine both have a fractional half when divided by two.

30
00:06:19,483 --> 00:06:25,688
Zeta at one half exists and is about minus one point four six.

31
00:06:25,688 --> 00:06:30,460
The critical line instead uses one half plus i t.

32
00:06:30,460 --> 00:06:32,369
Particular heights give zeros.

33
00:06:32,369 --> 00:06:36,664
Putting ninety-seven into t gives a nonzero complex value.

34
00:06:36,664 --> 00:06:41,914
Fixing the real part does not identify an integer as prime.

35
00:06:43,942 --> 00:06:50,839
With integer n used as height, ninety-seven and composite ninety-nine both occupy quadrant one.

36
00:06:50,839 --> 00:06:57,244
Comparing zeta at s, s plus one, and s minus one still fails.

37
00:06:57,244 --> 00:07:06,604
Matching ninety-seven's three colors keeps six primes and eighteen composites, while missing nineteen primes between two and one hundred.

38
00:07:08,608 --> 00:07:12,665
Removing the unit term gives zeta minus one.

39
00:07:12,665 --> 00:07:16,214
Its zeros are where zeta equals one.

40
00:07:16,214 --> 00:07:20,271
Multiplying the two functions combines both zero sets.

41
00:07:20,271 --> 00:07:27,370
Every hypothetical off-line zeta zero survives, so the switch does not prove the hypothesis.

42
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:33,949
Reordering the same finite sum leaves its value unchanged.

43
00:07:33,949 --> 00:07:41,025
For the tested coefficient reversal, subtracting the reverse makes opposite positions have opposite signs.

44
00:07:41,025 --> 00:07:45,574
A nonzero, nonnegative composite indicator cannot have that symmetry.

45
00:07:45,574 --> 00:07:51,134
Other definitions of a backward recurrence would need a separate derivation.

46
00:07:53,150 --> 00:07:59,133
Finite subtraction leaves nonprimes but requires knowing the prime terms to remove.

47
00:07:59,133 --> 00:08:04,120
At s equals minus one, the ordinary integer series diverges.

48
00:08:04,120 --> 00:08:12,098
Zeta's continued value, minus one twelfth, is not an ordinary sum for subtracting infinitely many primes.

49
00:08:14,108 --> 00:08:21,013
For real s greater than one, define A as the odd-number part of zeta.

50
00:08:21,013 --> 00:08:28,904
Then A minus one minus log A cancels every odd-prime coefficient and leaves weighted odd composites.

51
00:08:28,904 --> 00:08:34,329
The coefficient at ninety-seven is zero; at ninety-nine it is one.

52
00:08:34,329 --> 00:08:38,768
Evaluating H at ninety-seven does not extract that coefficient.

53
00:08:40,775 --> 00:08:46,224
Wilson's theorem tests a candidate without a list of previous primes.

54
00:08:46,224 --> 00:08:49,691
Our encoding places primes in quadrant one.

55
00:08:49,691 --> 00:08:54,150
The modular factorial supplies the answer; color displays it.

56
00:08:54,150 --> 00:08:56,131
This is not instantaneous.

57
00:08:56,131 --> 00:09:01,580
The separate four-divisor color test works only within its stated range.

58
00:09:03,608 --> 00:09:08,420
Removing trivial factor pairs leaves an exact composite remainder.

59
00:09:08,420 --> 00:09:16,973
For odd integers greater than one, D counts nontrivial pairs: zero for ninety-seven, two for ninety-nine.

60
00:09:16,973 --> 00:09:20,180
The proof covers that entire domain.

61
00:09:20,180 --> 00:09:24,991
The implementation was independently checked through one hundred thousand.

62
00:09:24,991 --> 00:09:27,664
It still performs divisor counting.

63
00:09:29,692 --> 00:09:33,309
The contribution is reproducible tests and counterexamples.

64
00:09:33,309 --> 00:09:39,511
Phase plots can guide searches; rigorous bounds must certify the zero count.

65
00:09:39,511 --> 00:09:44,162
Prime extraction also needs error bounds and cost comparisons.

66
00:09:44,162 --> 00:09:49,847
These experiments establish no new prime algorithm or Riemann Hypothesis advance.

67
00:09:51,858 --> 00:09:55,837
Start with a polynomial whose roots are distinct and real.

68
00:09:55,837 --> 00:10:01,408
As its coefficients follow a specified heat equation, those roots move and may collide.

69
00:10:01,408 --> 00:10:05,785
We can prove an interval during which no collision is possible.

70
00:10:05,785 --> 00:10:12,152
This is a guarantee about a finite mathematical system, with a checker that others can reuse.

71
00:10:15,858 --> 00:10:22,165
The recorded path began with four-color coordinates, moving origins, and phase measurements.

72
00:10:22,165 --> 00:10:27,946
An exactly solved quartic then exposed a failed local collision forecast.

73
00:10:27,946 --> 00:10:34,779
The next task kept the exterior-root interactions that the simpler estimate had discarded.

74
00:10:34,779 --> 00:10:42,137
Bounding those interactions led to the constant-diameter certificate, followed by the stronger contracting-diameter formula.

75
00:10:42,137 --> 00:10:48,970
The colors helped frame an investigation; the equations and counterexamples determined what survived.

76
00:10:50,983 --> 00:10:56,099
The records separate a proposal, its review, and its proof.

77
00:10:56,099 --> 00:11:00,703
Both bound formulas were supplied in the review contract.

78
00:11:00,703 --> 00:11:09,400
During review, Carbon retracted an incorrect product inequality and used reciprocal terms to justify the diameter estimate.

79
00:11:09,400 --> 00:11:15,027
Eventness marks the finite result complete and the infinite-system obligation open.

80
00:11:15,027 --> 00:11:23,212
Trace IDs make this history inspectable; a matching ID or reviewer agreement is not mathematical proof.

81
00:11:25,233 --> 00:11:30,806
The equation is P sub t equals minus P sub z z.

82
00:11:30,806 --> 00:11:34,521
We start at time zero and decrease t.

83
00:11:34,521 --> 00:11:40,558
For clarity, the animation uses positive elapsed duration u, equal to minus t.

84
00:11:40,558 --> 00:11:48,917
The polynomial evolution is a terminating derivative sum, so we can compute its coefficients exactly without Euler steps.

85
00:11:50,941 --> 00:11:52,610
Sort the initial roots.

86
00:11:52,610 --> 00:12:01,368
Record their number n, the smallest adjacent gap m, and the diameter D zero, from the leftmost root to the rightmost.

87
00:12:01,368 --> 00:12:06,372
Our running example has roots minus three, minus one, one, and three.

88
00:12:06,372 --> 00:12:12,211
There are four roots, the minimum gap is two, and the diameter is six.

89
00:12:14,941 --> 00:12:19,804
For these four roots, the evolving polynomial is shown here.

90
00:12:19,804 --> 00:12:25,640
Substituting w equals z squared reduces its root equation to a quadratic.

91
00:12:25,640 --> 00:12:31,476
That calculation proves the first collisions occur at elapsed duration two thirds.

92
00:12:31,476 --> 00:12:40,716
At that instant the polynomial becomes z squared minus one, squared: two double roots, at minus one and one.

93
00:12:42,733 --> 00:12:48,705
An isolated pair with gap m has squared gap m squared minus eight u.

94
00:12:48,705 --> 00:12:52,544
Its collision duration is exactly m squared over eight.

95
00:12:52,544 --> 00:12:58,942
In a larger system, this gives a conservative first guarantee using the smallest starting gap.

96
00:12:58,942 --> 00:13:02,355
For the quartic that duration is one half.

97
00:13:02,355 --> 00:13:05,767
The other roots let us improve this guarantee.

98
00:13:07,775 --> 00:13:13,155
Differentiate the equation saying that P is zero at a moving root.

99
00:13:13,155 --> 00:13:20,328
Factoring the polynomial then expresses that root's velocity as a sum over all the other roots.

100
00:13:20,328 --> 00:13:22,569
This is established heat-flow mathematics.

101
00:13:22,569 --> 00:13:31,088
The important change from a local guess is that the interactions outside the nearest pair remain in the calculation.

102
00:13:33,108 --> 00:13:39,079
Take adjacent roots a and b, and square their separation to obtain y.

103
00:13:39,079 --> 00:13:42,295
Subtracting their velocities gives this exact identity.

104
00:13:42,295 --> 00:13:46,888
The quantity S sums the influence of every exterior root.

105
00:13:46,888 --> 00:13:49,185
Adjacency makes each denominator positive.

106
00:13:49,185 --> 00:13:58,371
In elapsed time u, the minus eight term contracts the gap, while the interaction term offsets part of that contraction.

107
00:14:00,400 --> 00:14:09,478
As t decreases before a collision, the extreme roots move inward, so their diameter never exceeds its initial value.

108
00:14:09,478 --> 00:14:14,256
Each exterior-root denominator is therefore at most D zero squared.

109
00:14:14,256 --> 00:14:17,600
There are n minus two such terms.

110
00:14:17,600 --> 00:14:25,245
This gives a positive lower bound, kappa, for the interaction sum without solving every root trajectory.

111
00:14:27,275 --> 00:14:31,618
Solve the comparison equation starting from the smallest squared gap.

112
00:14:31,618 --> 00:14:35,526
Its value stays below every actual adjacent squared gap.

113
00:14:35,526 --> 00:14:40,304
The displayed duration T is where that lower bound reaches zero.

114
00:14:40,304 --> 00:14:42,909
Before T, every gap remains positive.

115
00:14:42,909 --> 00:14:50,727
For our example, T is about zero point five three zero zero two four, improving the one-half baseline.

116
00:14:52,733 --> 00:14:58,241
The velocity formulas initially apply only while roots are distinct and real.

117
00:14:58,241 --> 00:15:02,373
Suppose a first collision occurred before the guaranteed duration.

118
00:15:02,373 --> 00:15:05,586
The positive gap bound would forbid it.

119
00:15:05,586 --> 00:15:12,472
Roots cannot escape to infinity in finite time because the monic polynomial's coefficients stay bounded.

120
00:15:12,472 --> 00:15:16,603
Distinct real roots therefore continue throughout the claimed interval.

121
00:15:18,608 --> 00:15:20,702
We can retain more information.

122
00:15:20,702 --> 00:15:26,985
The extreme-root equations bound the rate at which the squared diameter shrinks in elapsed time.

123
00:15:26,985 --> 00:15:33,687
Writing d for its initial square, the diameter squared is at most d minus B u.

124
00:15:33,687 --> 00:15:39,970
This smaller denominator strengthens the lower bound on exterior interactions as time advances toward collision.

125
00:15:42,608 --> 00:15:46,900
Integrating the time-dependent comparison gives the stronger duration shown here.

126
00:15:46,900 --> 00:15:53,766
Gap packing guarantees that eta lies strictly between zero and one, so the expression is defined.

127
00:15:53,766 --> 00:15:57,629
The proof shows this duration exceeds the constant-diameter guarantee.

128
00:15:57,629 --> 00:16:03,637
For our quartic it is about zero point five three nine seven one nine.

129
00:16:03,637 --> 00:16:07,500
It remains a lower bound on the collision duration.

130
00:16:09,608 --> 00:16:14,008
Compare the three guaranteed durations with the exact quartic collision.

131
00:16:14,008 --> 00:16:21,487
The strongest certificate ends near zero point five four, but the actual collision is at two thirds.

132
00:16:21,487 --> 00:16:27,206
At duration zero point six, all four roots are still real and distinct.

133
00:16:27,206 --> 00:16:34,686
Being outside a certificate means that certificate is silent; it does not mean the polynomial has failed.

134
00:16:36,691 --> 00:16:43,318
Run the practical wrapper with an exact rational root list and a chosen duration.

135
00:16:43,318 --> 00:16:47,579
It constructs the polynomial and computes a rigorous bound.

136
00:16:47,579 --> 00:16:54,679
The report separates an interval guarantee from an exact root count at the selected time.

137
00:16:54,679 --> 00:16:57,993
For seventeen over thirty-two, both checks succeed.

138
00:16:57,993 --> 00:17:03,673
The decision uses rational enclosures, not a rounded decimal displayed on screen.

139
00:17:05,691 --> 00:17:12,193
Choose elapsed duration seventeen over thirty-two, exactly zero point five three one two five.

140
00:17:12,193 --> 00:17:19,623
It is beyond the constant-diameter guarantee but below the certified lower enclosure of the stronger duration.

141
00:17:19,623 --> 00:17:26,589
At the corresponding negative t, an exact Sturm count independently finds four distinct real roots.

142
00:17:26,589 --> 00:17:31,698
The theorem covers the whole interval; this count checks one sample.

143
00:17:33,733 --> 00:17:42,136
A root-tracking program can restrict its search to a duration below the certified lower bound, leaving a margin.

144
00:17:42,136 --> 00:17:44,937
The exact roots cannot collide there.

145
00:17:44,937 --> 00:17:50,073
This does not bound Euler error, Newton convergence, or floating-point error.

146
00:17:50,073 --> 00:18:00,343
Restarting at a later polynomial is mathematically possible, but its new root gaps need certification; this checker does not implement interval-root input.

147
00:18:02,358 --> 00:18:06,621
The quartic also provides a benchmark with a known answer.

148
00:18:06,621 --> 00:18:13,014
A frozen quadratic approximation at the origin predicts a collision there at duration nine twentieths.

149
00:18:13,014 --> 00:18:19,834
The exact polynomial at that point is instead two hundred forty-three over one hundred, not zero.

150
00:18:19,834 --> 00:18:26,228
This reveals omitted higher-order terms and gives a concrete test for any proposed improved predictor.

151
00:18:28,233 --> 00:18:32,459
Translate every root and the durations stay the same.

152
00:18:32,459 --> 00:18:39,972
Scale every root by a nonzero real factor a and the durations multiply by a squared.

153
00:18:39,972 --> 00:18:42,789
Repeated roots violate the starting assumptions.

154
00:18:42,789 --> 00:18:46,546
Degree two has its own exact pair formula.

155
00:18:46,546 --> 00:18:53,589
An arbitrary coefficient motion, or rounded measurements treated as exact roots, is a different problem.

156
00:18:55,608 --> 00:19:02,939
The Riemann entire function has a heat deformation called H sub t, with the same heat sign.

157
00:19:02,939 --> 00:19:08,976
Its zeros are all real exactly when t is at least a threshold, Lambda.

158
00:19:08,976 --> 00:19:12,857
RH is equivalent to Lambda being at most zero.

159
00:19:12,857 --> 00:19:16,738
Rodgers and Tao proved Lambda is at least zero.

160
00:19:16,738 --> 00:19:20,188
Thus RH is equivalent to Lambda equaling zero.

161
00:19:20,188 --> 00:19:22,775
Shared zero dynamics connect the questions.

162
00:19:25,608 --> 00:19:30,349
Our certificate uses finitely many roots and a finite diameter.

163
00:19:30,349 --> 00:19:40,305
The Riemann flow has infinitely many zeros, so an extension needs rigorous control of distant zeros and convergence of their interactions.

164
00:19:40,305 --> 00:19:45,520
Assuming every zero is real at time zero would assume RH.

165
00:19:45,520 --> 00:19:52,158
A uniform real-root interval below zero would contradict the known lower bound on Lambda.

166
00:19:52,158 --> 00:19:57,373
The open task is control toward zero from a known-real regime.

167
00:19:59,400 --> 00:20:10,161
Use the proof as a finite-system guarantee, the checker for exact rational examples, and the quartic to benchmark numerical or local approximations.

168
00:20:10,161 --> 00:20:18,477
The saved suite covers twenty-five root configurations and one hundred rational-time checks; those tests support the implementation.

169
00:20:18,477 --> 00:20:21,901
The general statement comes from the proof.

170
00:20:21,901 --> 00:20:28,750
Novelty remains unestablished, and no transfer to infinitely many Riemann zeros has been proved.

171
